Born in Safad[,] northern Palestine[, around] 1888[-]1890, Abbo[ was] a farmer and fisherman who later took up[ carp]entry and stone masonry. It was not until he moved to Berlin in 1911 to study sculpture that his artistic career took shape.
Ba[al]b[aki] has spent the last[ four] years finding[, and] determining[ attribution for,] 70 art[works] and 100 texts mentioning the artist.
Baalbaki says many of[ Ab]bo['s] sculptures[ didn]'t survive World War I. He believes those that were originally exhibited in museums[ in Germany] were taken during the war and melted down to make weapons.
Alongside Abbo['s] artworks are vintage photos of places he'd been or postcards from that time period, which Baalbaki says is to give an overall look at the life he led, in today's age of media and[ imagery].
